# Rank 1393 / CVE-2024-27308 (GHSA-r8w9-5wcg-vfj7, recorded CWE-416/CWE-672):
# tokio-rs/mio is the non-blocking IO layer under tokio. On Windows its
# `NamedPipe` is driven by an I/O completion port: each overlapped operation
# hands the kernel a raw pointer to one of three `Overlapped` fields embedded in
# a leaked `Arc<Inner>`, and the selector recovers the object from that pointer
# and dispatches to the field's callback. Readiness that is *synthesised* rather
# than completed -- an error stored into `io.read`, a write that finished
# inline, a fresh registration -- took a different route:
#
#   fn notify_readable(&self, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
#       if let Some(token) = self.token {                    // named_pipe.rs:941
#           let mut ev = Event::new(token);
#           ev.set_readable();
#           if let Some(events) = events {
#               events.push(ev);
#           } else {
#               let _ = self.cp.as_ref().unwrap().post(ev.to_completion_status());
#           }                                                // named_pipe.rs:949
#       }
#   }
#
# and `Event::to_completion_status` builds the packet with **no** overlapped:
#
#   CompletionStatus::new(self.flags, self.data as usize, std::ptr::null_mut())
#                                                            // event.rs:42
#
# A null `lpOverlapped` is the selector's "deliver verbatim" path --
# selector.rs:494 is `if iocp_event.overlapped().is_null() { events.push(
# Event::from_completion_status(iocp_event)) }` -- so the packet reaches the
# caller with no callback consulted and no object recovered.
#
# The weakness is a stale registration token, not a dangling Rust reference.
# `NamedPipe::deregister` (named_pipe.rs:643) only does `io.token = None`; it
# neither cancels nor drains packets sitting in the port's queue, and
# `Drop for NamedPipe` cancels the pending read and connect but likewise cannot
# recall a posted packet. So the token is captured when readiness is noticed and
# validated only then, while the packet is consumed an unbounded time later. In
# between, the pipe can be deregistered -- or dropped, since nothing holds a
# reference on `Inner` for the queued packet's lifetime -- and `Poll` can hand
# the same `Token` to a different source. The event is delivered anyway, and the
# consumer attributes readiness to whatever now owns that token: tokio then
# polls or wakes a resource that has been released. That is the CWE-672 reading;
# the CWE-416 label is the effect one level up, and inside mio itself no freed
# memory is ever dereferenced, because the packet carries no pointer at all.
#
# 90d4fe00df fixes it by giving the synthetic event the same machinery the real
# operations have: a fourth `event: Overlapped` field on `Inner` with an
# `event_done` callback and a `ptr_from_event_overlapped` un-projection, a new
# `Event::to_completion_status_with_overlapped` so the packet carries that
# field's address, `mem::forget(me.clone())` on a successful post so the packet
# owns a refcount, and -- the actual security content -- a second look at the
# registration when the packet comes back out:
#
#   let io = me.io.lock().unwrap();
#   if io.token.is_some() { ... events.push(ev) }              // named_pipe.rs:945
#
# The `data <<= 1; data += 1` shift is only a tag so the selector routes the
# packet as a named-pipe event and not an AFD poll result.
#
# The pool row is correct in every field: 90d4fe00df is a real, readable,
# non-merge commit on mio's 0.8.x maintenance line (it is the backport of
# 04c3bb6e, dc3b3f63 and 6fd5dd0d), it touches only src/sys/windows/event.rs and
# src/sys/windows/named_pipe.rs, and the vulnerable `notify_readable` /
# `notify_writable` / `to_completion_status` bodies are all present at its
# parent. No corpus correction.
#
# What fires today. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`
# reports 33 findings on the vulnerable checkout and 33 on the patched one,
# stable over three runs each -- VYQL-MEM-006 x22, VYQL-RACE-003 x9,
# VYQL-CRY-003 x2 -- and not one of them is in src/sys/windows/named_pipe.rs.
# The only row naming a named pipe at all is a code.WeakRandomValue on the
# random pipe name in tests/win_named_pipe.rs:31. The pattern ranks 1370, 1371
# and 1378 recorded holds again: VYQL-MEM-006 fires on
# src/sys/windows/selector.rs:500, which is the *non-null* overlapped branch, the
# one the fixed revision makes named pipes take. `vyql match` and `scan -flags with` over
# faithful repros of all three revisions below label nothing and emit no flag.
#
# Vocabulary is checked for reuse first, and the gap is wider than a binding.
# CWE-672 appears in vyql/taxonomy/cwe.json and in no concept or rule anywhere.
# The CWE-416 rule, VYQL-MEM-007, is `code.PointerFree reaches code.PointerUse`,
# and both concepts are bound only for C, C++ and Objective-C; they are the wrong
# vocabulary for Rust regardless, where what is released is an `Arc` refcount
# rather than an allocation. The nearest issue concept is
# code.ReentrantQueueCleanupReview (VYQL-MEM-019, CWE-415/416, C-only), whose
# condition is "queue cleanup and cancellation can both own queued entries
# without an observed reentrant cleanup guard" -- adjacent, but about a cleanup
# traversal racing a cancel, not about a queued notification outliving the
# registration it names. No concept and no rule ID are minted here, because the
# measurement below says nothing discriminating can be built on top of one.
#
# ATTENTION, no discrimination: every anchor a binding could key on is present
# unchanged in the patched tree, and the one that does move is the fixing
# commit's own naming. Counted with `vyql query -call` over both real checkouts:
#
#   CompletionStatus.new with a null overlapped   1 before (event.rs:42)
#                                                 1 after  (event.rs:42, same site)
#   CompletionPort::post in sys/windows           waker.rs:27, iocp.rs:255/256,
#                                                 named_pipe.rs -- before and after
#   ev.to_completion_status (mio-private)         3 before  1 after
#
# The documented API -- `CompletionStatus::new(bytes, token, lpOverlapped)`, and
# under it `PostQueuedCompletionStatus` -- scores 1 to 1 on the identical site,
# because the fixed revision never removes the null-overlapped constructor: the waker still
# needs it. Posting a packet with a null `lpOverlapped` is the ordinary IOCP
# wakeup idiom, documented as such, so labelling it is both non-discriminating
# and broad.
#
# The maximally repro-shaped key, mio's own `pub(super) to_completion_status`,
# goes 3 to 1 -- and the surviving site is case (c) below,
# src/sys/windows/waker.rs:27, whose body is the vulnerable idiom
# character-for-character (`Event::new(token)`, `set_readable()`,
# `port.post(ev.to_completion_status())`) and is correct in both revisions,
# because a `Waker`'s token is fixed for the life of the `Poll` and is never
# deregistered. So the crate contains a correct instance of the exact expression
# being detected, in the same module, before and after: 2 true and 1 false on the
# CVE's own tree, from a binding naming a `pub(super)` method that exists only
# inside mio and that no library documentation could produce. That is the
# genericity test in AGENTS.md failing outright.
#
# The two places the invariant is actually broken are byte-identical across the
# fix: `deregister` clearing `io.token` without cancelling in-flight posts, and
# selector.rs:494 pushing a null-overlapped packet with no registration re-check.
# The fixed revision works by never taking that branch for named pipes, not by changing it.
# What is left as a separator is the existence of `schedule_event` / `event_done`
# and the `mem::forget(me.clone())` inside them -- the fixing commit's own
# layout, which is standing rejection grounds.
#
# The delegated-check family is tested, not assumed, in both directions. Rank
# 1380's counterweight comes out negative: the patched `notify_readable` retains
# only `Event::new`, `set_readable` and `events.push`, every one of which the
# vulnerable form also has, so the fixed revision leaves no API-documented call inside the
# method it guards to key on -- the call that moved, `post`, is still made one
# frame down. And rank 1364's resolution half does not hold on this tree:
# `self.schedule_event` is not among the 2983 unresolved call sites mio reports,
# so the delegated call resolves. Landing the caller-side control attribution
# ranks 1364, 1372 and 1377 are blocked on would therefore not unblock this rank
# -- with it, both revisions attribute the same `post` to `notify_readable`. No
# new engine gap is filed.
#
# The three cases are the pair, plus the instance that makes it a pair. (a) and
# (b) are the two real revisions and record the miss. (c) is the untouched
# `Waker::wake`: it must stay a reject, and it is the reason (a) cannot be turned
# into an expect by keying on the idiom.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-no-discrimination, all three revisions
# rejected. (c) is correct today and must remain a reject.

test "rank1393: (a) a named-pipe readiness event is posted to the completion port with no overlapped, so the token it captured is delivered whether or not the pipe is still registered"
  lang rust
  file src/sys/windows/named_pipe.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-019
  reject VYQL-RACE-003
  code
  ```
  use std::io;
  use std::mem;
  use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;
  use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

  use crate::event::Event;
  use crate::sys::windows::iocp::{CompletionPort, CompletionStatus};
  use crate::sys::windows::{Handle, Overlapped};
  use crate::{Registry, Token};
  use windows_sys::Win32::System::IO::{OVERLAPPED, OVERLAPPED_ENTRY};

  struct Inner {
      connect: Overlapped,
      read: Overlapped,
      write: Overlapped,
      handle: Handle,
      connecting: AtomicBool,
      io: Mutex<Io>,
  }

  struct Io {
      cp: Option<Arc<CompletionPort>>,
      token: Option<Token>,
      read: State,
      write: State,
      connect_error: Option<io::Error>,
  }

  impl NamedPipe {
      fn deregister(&mut self, registry: &Registry) -> io::Result<()> {
          let mut io = self.inner.io.lock().unwrap();
          io.check_association(registry, true)?;
          if io.token.is_none() {
              return Err(io::Error::new(
                  io::ErrorKind::NotFound,
                  "I/O source not registered with `Registry`",
              ));
          }
          io.token = None;
          Ok(())
      }
  }

  fn read_done(status: &OVERLAPPED_ENTRY, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
      let status = CompletionStatus::from_entry(status);
      let me = unsafe { Arc::from_raw(Inner::ptr_from_read_overlapped(status.overlapped())) };
      let mut io = me.io.lock().unwrap();
      let mut buf = match mem::replace(&mut io.read, State::None) {
          State::Pending(buf, _) => buf,
          _ => unreachable!(),
      };
      unsafe {
          match me.result(status.overlapped()) {
              Ok(n) => {
                  buf.set_len(status.bytes_transferred() as usize);
                  io.read = State::Ok(buf, 0);
              }
              Err(e) => {
                  io.read = State::Err(e);
              }
          }
      }
      io.notify_readable(events);
  }

  impl Io {
      fn notify_readable(&self, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
          if let Some(token) = self.token {
              let mut ev = Event::new(token);
              ev.set_readable();

              if let Some(events) = events {
                  events.push(ev);
              } else {
                  let _ = self.cp.as_ref().unwrap().post(ev.to_completion_status());
              }
          }
      }

      fn notify_writable(&self, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
          if let Some(token) = self.token {
              let mut ev = Event::new(token);
              ev.set_writable();

              if let Some(events) = events {
                  events.push(ev);
              } else {
                  let _ = self.cp.as_ref().unwrap().post(ev.to_completion_status());
              }
          }
      }
  }

  impl Event {
      pub(super) fn to_completion_status(&self) -> CompletionStatus {
          CompletionStatus::new(self.flags, self.data as usize, std::ptr::null_mut())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1393: (b) the fixed revision routes the same event through an owned overlapped whose callback re-reads the registration before delivering it"
  lang rust
  file src/sys/windows/named_pipe.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-019
  reject VYQL-RACE-003
  code
  ```
  use std::io;
  use std::mem;
  use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

  use crate::event::Event;
  use crate::sys::windows::iocp::{CompletionPort, CompletionStatus};
  use crate::sys::windows::Overlapped;
  use crate::Token;
  use windows_sys::Win32::System::IO::{OVERLAPPED, OVERLAPPED_ENTRY};

  struct Inner {
      connect: Overlapped,
      read: Overlapped,
      write: Overlapped,
      event: Overlapped,
      io: Mutex<Io>,
  }

  impl Inner {
      unsafe fn ptr_from_event_overlapped(ptr: *mut OVERLAPPED) -> *const Inner {
          (ptr as *mut Overlapped).wrapping_sub(3) as *const Inner
      }
  }

  fn event_done(status: &OVERLAPPED_ENTRY, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
      let status = CompletionStatus::from_entry(status);
      let me = unsafe { Arc::from_raw(Inner::ptr_from_event_overlapped(status.overlapped())) };

      let io = me.io.lock().unwrap();

      if io.token.is_some() {
          if let Some(events) = events {
              let mut ev = Event::from_completion_status(&status);
              ev.data >>= 1;
              events.push(ev);
          }
      }
  }

  impl Io {
      fn notify_readable(&self, me: &Arc<Inner>, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
          if let Some(token) = self.token {
              let mut ev = Event::new(token);
              ev.set_readable();

              if let Some(events) = events {
                  events.push(ev);
              } else {
                  self.schedule_event(me, ev);
              }
          }
      }

      fn notify_writable(&self, me: &Arc<Inner>, events: Option<&mut Vec<Event>>) {
          if let Some(token) = self.token {
              let mut ev = Event::new(token);
              ev.set_writable();

              if let Some(events) = events {
                  events.push(ev);
              } else {
                  self.schedule_event(me, ev);
              }
          }
      }

      fn schedule_event(&self, me: &Arc<Inner>, mut event: Event) {
          event.data <<= 1;
          event.data += 1;

          let completion_status =
              event.to_completion_status_with_overlapped(me.event.as_ptr() as *mut _);

          match self.cp.as_ref().unwrap().post(completion_status) {
              Ok(_) => {
                  mem::forget(me.clone());
              }
              Err(_) => {}
          }
      }
  }

  impl Event {
      pub(super) fn to_completion_status(&self) -> CompletionStatus {
          CompletionStatus::new(self.flags, self.data as usize, std::ptr::null_mut())
      }

      pub(super) fn to_completion_status_with_overlapped(
          &self,
          overlapped: *mut Overlapped,
      ) -> CompletionStatus {
          CompletionStatus::new(self.flags, self.data as usize, overlapped)
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1393: (c) the waker posts the same null-overlapped packet correctly, on a token that is never deregistered, and is unchanged at both revisions"
  lang rust
  file src/sys/windows/waker.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-019
  reject VYQL-RACE-003
  code
  ```
  use crate::sys::windows::iocp::CompletionPort;
  use crate::sys::windows::{Event, Selector};
  use crate::Token;
  use std::io;
  use std::sync::Arc;

  pub struct Waker {
      token: Token,
      port: Arc<CompletionPort>,
  }

  impl Waker {
      pub fn new(selector: &Selector, token: Token) -> io::Result<Waker> {
          Ok(Waker {
              token,
              port: selector.clone_port(),
          })
      }

      pub fn wake(&self) -> io::Result<()> {
          let mut ev = Event::new(self.token);
          ev.set_readable();

          self.port.post(ev.to_completion_status())
      }
  }
  ```
